WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.290
Speaker 1: One of the most popular sort of
punching bags for conservatives right now

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.950
is the state of California.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.970
They love to say California is a liberal hellhole.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:14.890
California is what happens when the left wing
policies of Democrats run rampant.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:16.470
But the truth is that that's bogus.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:22.080
And a few weeks ago, I did a story called
so-called failed state of California sets

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.340
economic growth record.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.000
And in that story, I went through the economic
data and it turns out California is actually

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.189
doing really well.

00:00:29.189 --> 00:00:32.119
Economic growth is some of the highest in
the US and California.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:36.680
The economic surplus in California, the budget
surplus is at a record level.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:40.420
So those claims about California throughout
the pandemic have been false.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.079
Another talking point is people are fleeing
California.

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:45.079
Everybody's leaving California.

00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:46.260
It's going to be left empty.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.159
The cupboards will be bare of people, as Trump
likes to say.

00:00:50.159 --> 00:00:53.280
We now have a new study about that.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.559
And it is also just a lie.

00:00:55.559 --> 00:00:59.799
Republicans want to paint California as a
place that people are just desperate to get

00:00:59.799 --> 00:01:01.129
out of, they'll go anywhere.

00:01:01.129 --> 00:01:04.269
They'll just get me out of California.

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:10.420
And it never passed the sniff test because
if people really were getting out there, magical,

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:17.820
perfect, rational market would lead to a significant
drop in the cost of living in California.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:22.450
Basic supply and demand rents would go down,
property values would go down.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:23.729
That's not happening.

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:28.369
But putting the sniff test aside, there's
a new study from the University of California

00:01:28.369 --> 00:01:33.880
system which found that the number of people
moving out of California into other states

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.510
is up slightly compared to 2012.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:40.350
But it's at very similar numbers to what we
saw throughout the mid and late 2000s.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:45.409
There are some people moving out as people
move that move out of any state, but at historically

00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:46.469
sort of normal levels.

00:01:46.469 --> 00:01:51.149
Meanwhile, there are still young people moving
into California at relatively high rates.

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:52.289
They're also going other places.

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:55.319
Some of them are going to Texas, some of them
are going to Colorado.

00:01:55.319 --> 00:02:01.289
You do see some older folks moving out of
California, but it's really no big deal.

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:06.700
The percentage of Californians who plan to
leave the state when you ask them has remained

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.180
basically the same over the last two years.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:14.260
Twenty three percent of California California
voters say I am seriously considering leaving

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.260
the state.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.319
That was twenty four percent two years ago.

00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:18.620
Basically the same.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:19.620
That sounds huge.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:25.409
And obviously, if a quarter of California
left California, it would be a cataclysm economically.

00:02:25.409 --> 00:02:28.269
But remember that most people who say they're
considering leaving don't.

00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:32.989
I mean, I've been considering moving to Miami
for five years and then I realize I don't

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:34.519
really know anybody there.

00:02:34.519 --> 00:02:35.720
I'd be far from my family.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.110
I'm going to stay put, at least for now.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:42.890
But like if you ask me at many points, I would
consider leaving and going to Florida.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:43.890
Sure.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:44.890
Yeah, that's something I've considered.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:45.890
I'm not really going to do it.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:47.129
And that's for most people.

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:50.200
One interesting thing is that there's a big
partizan gap, though, on that question.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.590
If you ask Democrats.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:57.099
Twenty one percent say I'm considering leaving
California asked Republicans, 30 percent say

00:02:57.099 --> 00:02:59.060
I'm considering leaving California.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:04.159
No surprise politically, it's very in vogue
right now to say you're thinking of leaving

00:03:04.159 --> 00:03:05.159
California.

00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:07.690
And guys, I'll do it.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:11.930
Don't make me because I'm just one day from
renting a U-Haul and getting out of here.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:12.950
Most people don't do it.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:18.330
And it's important to remember that if people
did leave California in large numbers, the

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:24.810
heroic, perfect market that the Republicans
love to harp on would lead to lower prices.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.239
That's what Republicans claim is the problem
in California.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:32.959
They say prices are too high, a real estate
cost too much rent is too damn high.

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:34.799
That's caused by demand.

00:03:34.799 --> 00:03:39.640
There's a reason it's more expensive to live
in San Francisco than it is to live in, you

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:46.780
know, Tulsa, Oklahoma, no offense to Oklahoma,
to education is better in California.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:49.209
There are higher salaries in California than
in Oklahoma.

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:50.920
There's more access to culture.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:54.200
There's more access to airports with direct
flights to the rest of the world.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.710
There's a higher standard of health care.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:57.700
There's better infrastructure.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.110
Demand drives prices up.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.860
Doesn't mean people in Oklahoma are bad people.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.930
It's just the market that these right wingers
point to.

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.519
That's why it's expensive to be in California.

00:04:07.519 --> 00:04:12.310
But right wingers just feel like everybody's
leaving California.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:15.500
So do we pay attention to facts or do we pay
attention to feelings?

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.930
I forget how that statement goes.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:22.300
Over time, people may leave California, who
knows?

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:26.750
But it's just not happening right now to any
significant degree.

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:30.530
California's not failing economically and
people are not fleeing California.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:31.800
That's the truth.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.861
It may change in the future, but it's not
happening right now

